What Are Separation Membranes for Biogas Purification?
Separation membranes for biogas purification are specialized materials that separate gases based on differences in their ability to pass through a membrane. Raw biogas typically contains methane and carbon dioxide, along with impurities such as water vapor, hydrogen sulfide, and trace contaminants.

Membrane systems help remove carbon dioxide and selected impurities to increase methane concentration. The resulting gas can be used for energy production and, following the required treatment and quality checks, injected into gas networks or used as vehicle fuel.

The technology is attractive because membrane modules can be arranged in stages to meet different purification requirements. System performance depends on membrane selectivity, operating pressure, feed-gas composition, pretreatment, and maintenance practices.

